course: dictionary
lesson: 7275
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. The word pinch is a good example. «Lick a lemon and it is sour; taste a tiny pinch of baking soda and it is bitter and soapy.» Here a pinch is a very small amount that you can hold between your finger and thumb.
explain: The same word can also mean squeezing. «Hamid gets his cheeks pinched by about 20 aunties» means they pressed his cheeks tightly between their fingers and thumbs. Before you choose a meaning, ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: Watch for phrases too. «Take it with a pinch of salt» does not mean add salt. It means do not fully believe it. The words around a phrase give you the clue, not just the one word.
fact: Pinch can be a verb (to pinch something) or a noun (a pinch of something).
fact: To pinch means to press something tightly between your thumb and finger, sometimes in a way that hurts.
fact: A pinch is a very small amount, like «a pinch of salt».
fact: Informal British: to pinch means to steal something small, as in «someone pinched my rubber».
fact: «At a pinch» (UK) and «in a pinch» (US) mean if really needed.
exercise: «Lick a lemon and it is sour; taste a tiny pinch of baking soda and it is bitter and soapy.» In this sentence, what is a pinch? | A kind of soap | A big bowl of soup | *A very small amount you hold between finger and thumb | A lemon that has gone off
exercise: Someone shouts, «Someone pinched my rubber!» What does pinched mean? | *Stole something small | Squeezed very hard | Lost it in the rain | Painted it bright red
exercise: What does «at a pinch» mean? | Not at all | Somewhere in the kitchen | Very quickly | *If really needed
test: «Take it with a pinch of salt» means what? | Add more salt to it | *Do not fully believe it | Put it in a jar | Make it salty
test: «These shoes pinch, so I can't walk far.» What does pinch mean here? | Make a loud noise | Give a small gift | Are brand new and shiny | *Are too tight and hurt
exam: Which meaning fits «These shoes pinch, so I can't walk far»? | Very loud | Brand new | A very small amount | *Too tight and hurting
